WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.000
But I will do away with life and die like a plan of combat.

00:15.000 --> 00:30.200
I'm going to get it.

00:30.200 --> 00:31.200
So loin steak.

00:31.200 --> 00:32.200
Ham hock.

00:32.200 --> 00:33.200
Roast beef.

00:33.200 --> 00:34.200
Pork shoulder.

00:34.200 --> 00:40.420
Oh, the mutton chops and chickens must be in your bag, Harriet.

00:40.420 --> 00:41.420
Dig them out.

00:41.420 --> 00:42.420
Yes, sir.

00:42.420 --> 00:46.620
But I still say I think we ought to buy our own home freezing unit, Daddy.

00:46.620 --> 00:52.220
I don't think it's right of you to sneak around here hiding our meat in the school cafeteria freezer.

00:52.220 --> 00:54.020
What's wrong with it?

00:54.020 --> 00:58.420
Does it cost the school anything extra to freeze my meat along with its own?

00:58.420 --> 00:59.820
Not a penny.

00:59.820 --> 01:01.420
But the inconvenience.

01:01.420 --> 01:03.620
What inconvenience?

01:03.620 --> 01:07.020
After school, I simply decide what we're going to have for dinner,

01:07.020 --> 01:08.920
remove it from this freezer,

01:08.920 --> 01:12.220
and by the time I get it home, it's nicely defrosted.

01:12.220 --> 01:15.820
Now, uh, hand me my mutton chops and hush.

01:15.820 --> 01:20.320
Mother thinks we ought to buy that freezing unit the man showed us in the store yesterday.

01:20.320 --> 01:23.220
For $380?

01:23.220 --> 01:28.020
It would be cheaper to send our meat to the North Pole.

01:28.020 --> 01:31.320
Here are the mutton chops, Daddy. Let's get it over with.

01:31.320 --> 01:33.920
All right, now, you stand guard, Harriet.

01:33.920 --> 01:37.020
If any other faculty members catch on to my little dodge,

01:37.020 --> 01:40.720
they're liable to demand equal rights for their meat.

01:40.720 --> 01:43.520
Now, I wouldn't want to be crowded out of a good thing.

01:43.520 --> 01:46.720
That keeps the dogs from the goblins.

01:46.720 --> 01:47.720
Hold it, Daddy.

01:47.720 --> 01:48.820
Oh, somebody coming?

01:48.820 --> 01:51.520
I'll be right.

01:51.520 --> 01:52.620
Good morning, Miss Brooks.

01:52.620 --> 01:54.620
Morning, Harriet. Mr. Conklin?

01:54.620 --> 01:58.120
The custodian's ill, so Walter and I are making his rounds for him.

01:58.120 --> 02:01.520
Yeah, we just wanted to check and make certain the freezer door is closed.

02:01.520 --> 02:04.220
What brings you down here, sir?

02:04.220 --> 02:06.520
Who, me?

02:06.520 --> 02:09.720
Well, uh, I, uh...

02:09.720 --> 02:15.820
Well, I'm very proud of the fact that my school has one of the finest freezers in the country.

02:15.820 --> 02:20.120
So I frequently come down here to inspect it.

02:20.120 --> 02:22.420
Yes, indeed.

02:22.420 --> 02:25.720
This freezer is very close to my heart.

02:25.720 --> 02:29.120
Maybe that's why his heart is so...

02:29.120 --> 02:32.220
It is a dandy freezer, Mr. Conklin.

02:32.220 --> 02:35.320
Harriet, aren't you supposed to be hall monitor this morning?

02:35.320 --> 02:36.720
Golly, I forgot!

02:36.720 --> 02:39.220
Oh, excuse me, Daddy, but I'm late.

02:39.220 --> 02:43.220
Oh, by the way, Miss Brooks, we saw Mr. Boynton in the appliance store yesterday,

02:43.220 --> 02:45.220
and he bought himself a new stove.

02:45.220 --> 02:47.720
He's got four burners.

02:47.720 --> 02:52.320
So have I, but he never turns them on.

02:52.320 --> 02:54.820
He doesn't like to cook at my place.

02:54.820 --> 02:57.520
Mother sent Daddy and me over there to buy a home freezer,

02:57.520 --> 03:00.220
but Daddy figures it's cheaper if we take our meals...

03:00.220 --> 03:03.320
On your horse!

03:03.320 --> 03:08.020
Hi, everybody.

03:08.020 --> 03:11.120
What's in the basket, Miss Conklin?

03:11.120 --> 03:14.820
My lunch.

03:14.820 --> 03:18.320
Now then, Miss Brooks, it is with considerable horror

03:18.320 --> 03:22.820
that I recall the last time you pinch-hit for our custodian.

03:22.820 --> 03:26.320
You repaired some door knobs, remember?

03:26.320 --> 03:28.620
I remember. Every time you went to open a door,

03:28.620 --> 03:31.220
the knob came away in your hand and you fell flat.

03:31.220 --> 03:34.120
Quiet!

03:34.120 --> 03:38.120
I trust you will have mercy on me today, Miss Brooks.

03:38.120 --> 03:43.120
You see, the Board of Education has asked that I report for my annual physical examination,

03:43.120 --> 03:46.720
and I would like to get there in fairly good shape.

03:46.720 --> 03:48.720
Well, you can depend on me, Mr. Conklin.

03:48.720 --> 03:52.220
I'll stay as far away from you as possible, starting immediately.

03:52.220 --> 03:54.420
Come on, Walter. Bye, Miss Conklin.

03:54.420 --> 04:04.320
Goodbye.

04:04.320 --> 04:05.720
Why bother going back?

04:05.720 --> 04:08.320
Well, it never hurts to recheck, Walter.

04:08.320 --> 04:20.320
You see, it was open.

04:20.320 --> 04:21.320
Going to lunch, Walter?

04:21.320 --> 04:22.220
Yes, ma'am.

04:22.220 --> 04:25.220
I just asked Harriet to join me, but she's upset about her old man.

04:25.220 --> 04:29.420
Mr. Conklin's been gone since early this morning.

04:29.420 --> 04:30.920
Hasn't he been in his office?

04:30.920 --> 04:35.320
No, ma'am. Harriet hasn't seen Hyde in her scalp of him.

04:35.320 --> 04:36.520
Well, that's funny.

04:36.520 --> 04:40.220
If he had an off-campus appointment, surely he'd have told Harriet about it.

04:40.220 --> 04:41.820
No, no, not necessarily.

04:41.820 --> 04:43.220
She had a little argument with him this morning,

04:43.220 --> 04:45.620
and Mr. Conklin got pretty hot under the collar.

04:45.620 --> 04:46.820
Oh.

04:46.820 --> 04:51.220
Well, he's probably cooled off by now.

04:51.220 --> 04:53.220
I don't think there's any cause for concern, Walter.

04:53.220 --> 04:55.620
He'll turn up sooner or later.

04:55.620 --> 04:57.820
He always does.

04:57.820 --> 05:01.520
It's the old story, every silver lining has a cloud.

05:01.520 --> 05:03.720
Are you ready for the biology lab, Miss Brooks?

05:03.720 --> 05:05.120
Guilty, Your Honor.

05:05.120 --> 05:07.920
I haven't seen much of Mr. Boynton lately, you know.

05:07.920 --> 05:10.920
His time is being pretty thoroughly monopolized.

05:10.920 --> 05:13.520
Yeah, I know. By Miss Daisy Enright.

05:13.520 --> 05:16.520
I understand she's been knitting things for the bashful one.

05:16.520 --> 05:18.220
Sweaters and socks.

05:18.220 --> 05:19.320
What's her angle?

05:19.320 --> 05:20.620
Oh, that's obvious.

05:20.620 --> 05:25.620
She's dropping stitches and subtle hints about what a handy housewife she'd make.

05:25.620 --> 05:28.420
Her game is as old as the hills, Walter.

05:28.420 --> 05:30.720
More than one can play it, though.

05:30.720 --> 05:32.020
What do you mean?

05:32.020 --> 05:34.720
Harriet gave you an opening you could drive a truck through, Miss Brooks.

05:34.720 --> 05:37.820
She said that Mr. Boynton bought a new stove yesterday, right?

05:37.820 --> 05:38.720
Right.

05:38.720 --> 05:41.120
Well, you just listened to old Dr. Cupid.

05:41.120 --> 05:44.620
Now, I ask you, who is the handy little potential housewife

05:44.620 --> 05:50.220
who is going to volunteer to cook the first meal on Mr. Boynton's new stove?

05:50.220 --> 05:53.020
I have a lady doctor.

05:53.020 --> 05:55.820
Oh, what are you waiting for? Go get something simmering.

05:55.820 --> 05:57.720
And don't spare the lard.

05:57.720 --> 06:02.220
If this works out, Doc, we'll name our first baby lamb chop after you.

06:02.220 --> 06:08.620
Oh, yeah.

06:08.620 --> 06:13.420
I don't know, Mr. Boynton. It's a little snug right through there.

06:13.420 --> 06:16.520
I'd like to let it out.

06:16.520 --> 06:19.020
Hi.

06:19.020 --> 06:20.820
Hi.

06:20.820 --> 06:24.420
Speaking of things I'd like to let out, how are you, Miss Brooks?

06:24.420 --> 06:27.020
How do you like this sweater Miss Enright made for me?

06:27.020 --> 06:30.920
Doesn't it look like something out of Esquire?

06:30.920 --> 06:34.420
Out of Esquire by Seabiscuit.

06:34.420 --> 06:36.420
Oh, you're such a doll.

06:36.420 --> 06:38.620
But then, you know, we see each other so seldom

06:38.620 --> 06:43.920
that just the sight of you gives me the feeling that it's old home week.

06:43.920 --> 06:46.820
A very old home week.

06:46.820 --> 06:51.020
Oh, thanks, Miss Enright. There's no reason we shouldn't see more of each other.

06:51.020 --> 06:55.720
After all, science has discovered antidotes for everything these days.

06:55.720 --> 06:56.420
Antidotes?

06:56.420 --> 06:58.020
Oh, she's so playful, Mr. Boynton.

06:58.020 --> 07:01.520
Suppose you show her the gloves I knitted you and the hanky I made.

07:01.520 --> 07:03.820
Oh, they're right here.

07:03.820 --> 07:07.920
This isn't my birthday or anything, but Miss Enright explained being the domestic type,

07:07.920 --> 07:10.320
she likes to keep busy making things.

07:10.320 --> 07:14.320
Oh, I think that's a wonderful quality in a girl. Don't you?

07:14.320 --> 07:18.120
In a girl, yes.

07:18.120 --> 07:20.420
That really is a lovely handkerchief, though.

07:20.420 --> 07:23.920
Thank you, darling. It's all hand-sewn.

07:23.920 --> 07:27.820
Yes, indeedy, I did it all with my own little needle.

07:27.820 --> 07:31.820
Oh, do you use a needle?

07:31.820 --> 07:35.620
I thought maybe you just threaded your own claws.

07:35.620 --> 07:36.720
Claws? Now, just a...

07:36.720 --> 07:39.420
Don't pay any attention to her, Mr. Boynton.

07:39.420 --> 07:44.420
She probably got up on the wrong side of her broom this morning.

07:44.420 --> 07:46.320
Well, I have to be running along.

07:46.320 --> 07:51.420
I understand you have a lunch date with Mr. Boynton, so I presume you want to be alone.

07:51.420 --> 07:52.520
Well, I don't know.

07:52.520 --> 07:55.620
I do. Bye.

07:55.620 --> 07:58.920
Oh, and by the way, Mr. Boynton, don't forget to drop by one night,

07:58.920 --> 08:03.020
and while I'm doing my knitting, you can enjoy my brand-new television set.

08:03.020 --> 08:04.720
You know, there's some fabulous shows.

08:04.720 --> 08:07.620
Oh, I know, and with some great stars, too.

08:07.620 --> 08:11.820
Gosh, a few years ago, when I bought my first set with a 10-inch screen,

08:11.820 --> 08:13.320
there wasn't anything to watch but puppets.

08:13.320 --> 08:15.620
Oh, yes, I know. I know just what you mean.

08:15.620 --> 08:20.120
Yes, in those days, I remember when I was in my teens,

08:20.120 --> 08:23.720
there weren't very many stars on television.

08:23.720 --> 08:27.620
When you were in your teens, there weren't many stars on the flag.

08:27.620 --> 08:29.620
Oh!

08:29.620 --> 08:31.420
I'm surprised at you, Miss Brooks.

08:31.420 --> 08:33.020
I don't think you were very civil.

08:33.020 --> 08:36.020
Who's civil in the Civil War?

08:36.020 --> 08:38.820
Oh, let's forget about Barbara Fritchie.

08:38.820 --> 08:40.520
How do you feel about lunch?

08:40.520 --> 08:45.020
Well, frankly, it's been so warm in here all morning, I'd settle for something cool,

08:45.020 --> 08:47.620
but all I could handle would be an ice cream cone.

08:47.620 --> 08:49.620
An ice cream cone?

08:49.620 --> 08:53.620
Well, fine, that'll leave you with a big appetite for the dinner I'm going to cook for you.

08:53.620 --> 08:55.620
Oh, I'm sorry, Miss Brooks.

08:55.620 --> 08:59.620
I don't know if I can do that. I'm a little busy.

08:59.620 --> 09:03.620
Well, fine, that'll leave you with a big appetite for the dinner I'm going to cook for you.

09:03.620 --> 09:05.620
You want to cook dinner for me?

09:05.620 --> 09:10.620
Well, why not? Being a domestic type, I love to cook dinner for somebody,

09:10.620 --> 09:13.620
especially somebody like a man.

09:13.620 --> 09:17.620
But, you know, I wish I could cook dinner on a new stove just once.

09:17.620 --> 09:20.620
Say, it's odd that you should mention that.

09:20.620 --> 09:22.620
I bought a new stove just yesterday.

09:22.620 --> 09:28.620
Oh, I mean it. I even borrowed Mr. Cochran's tool chest so that I could install it myself.

09:28.620 --> 09:30.620
It's all set up in my kitchen.

09:30.620 --> 09:32.620
Oh, isn't that the most amazing coincidence?

09:32.620 --> 09:35.620
You actually mean you have a new stove in your kitchen?

09:35.620 --> 09:39.620
May I never lead another troop of Boy Scouts to Crystal Lake?

09:39.620 --> 09:41.620
Well, I tell you what I'm going to do.

09:41.620 --> 09:45.620
I'm going to forget about the stove I was thinking of and cook dinner on your new stove.

09:45.620 --> 09:48.620
And it won't cost you a thing, Mr. Boynton. I'm going to do all the shopping.

09:48.620 --> 09:52.620
Oh, well, no. I don't want to put you to a lot of expense, Miss Brooks,

09:52.620 --> 09:54.620
particularly since I could have dinner on Mr. Conklin.

09:54.620 --> 09:56.620
On Mr. Conklin?

09:56.620 --> 10:00.620
Well, yes. I bought him lunch yesterday, so he reciprocated by inviting me to dinner at his place tonight.

10:00.620 --> 10:03.620
Well, tell him you'll take a rain check.

10:03.620 --> 10:05.620
All right. I'll do it.

10:05.620 --> 10:07.620
Oh, you won't find him in his office right now.

10:07.620 --> 10:09.620
For some reason, he's been missing ever since...

10:09.620 --> 10:10.620
Miss Brooks!

10:10.620 --> 10:11.620
Yeah?

10:11.620 --> 10:13.620
Miss Brooks, you've done it again!

10:13.620 --> 10:14.620
I've done what again, Walter?

10:14.620 --> 10:23.620
Well, while I was making the noon rounds for our custodian, I distinctly heard our beloved principal calling for help from inside the cafeteria freezer!

10:25.620 --> 10:27.620
Inside the freezer?

10:27.620 --> 10:29.620
Well, why didn't you let him out?

10:29.620 --> 10:31.620
What? And get myself killed?

10:32.620 --> 10:36.620
I mean, I thought you deserved the honor of springing him, Miss Brooks.

10:36.620 --> 10:44.620
Walter, you're a gentleman and a coward.

10:49.620 --> 10:51.620
I can't hear anything.

10:51.620 --> 10:54.620
Walter could have been mistaken. Maybe it was just his imagination.

10:54.620 --> 11:04.620
His imagination's catching pneumonia.

11:04.620 --> 11:20.620
Well, you said you wanted an ice cream cone. Take him.

11:35.620 --> 11:37.620
Keep your distance!

11:37.620 --> 11:39.620
What do you want?

11:39.620 --> 11:42.620
I just heard that you were defrosted, sir.

11:42.620 --> 11:46.620
I came to pay my respects. I brought you some flowers.

11:46.620 --> 11:48.620
Don't rush me.

11:51.620 --> 11:55.620
No thanks to you, I am still among the living.

11:55.620 --> 11:59.620
Please, Mr. Conklin, I've come to praise you, not to bury you.

11:59.620 --> 12:04.620
That is, I wanted to wish you a speedy recovery, sir.

12:04.620 --> 12:06.620
Bless you.

12:06.620 --> 12:10.620
I picked these flowers for you myself. Just smell them.

12:10.620 --> 12:13.620
Are you kidding?

12:14.620 --> 12:22.620
After a four-hour stretch in that freezer, my smeller couldn't distinguish a petunia from a pickle.

12:22.620 --> 12:27.620
I'm sorry, sir, but how was I to know you were inside when I closed it?

12:27.620 --> 12:30.620
May I ask what you were doing in the freezer?

12:30.620 --> 12:32.620
You may.

12:32.620 --> 12:35.620
What were you doing in the freezer?

12:35.620 --> 12:37.620
Freezing.

12:40.620 --> 12:46.620
Now then, Miss Brooks, I should like to remind you that I have to take my physical exam tomorrow,

12:46.620 --> 12:50.620
along with all other high school principals in the area.

12:50.620 --> 12:55.620
But you won't have whipped your cold by then, Mr. Conklin. Can't you postpone your exam?

12:55.620 --> 12:59.620
And miss the opportunity of a lifetime? No, indeed.

12:59.620 --> 13:08.620
The head of the board has decreed that all local principals are to convene once a month to exchange ideas on operational procedure.

13:08.620 --> 13:15.620
These meetings will be presided over by that principal whom Mr. Stone will select tomorrow afternoon.

13:15.620 --> 13:17.620
After the physical exam?

13:17.620 --> 13:22.620
Precisely. And since the job carries with it a great deal of extra work,

13:22.620 --> 13:27.620
not to mention prestige and a bit of added remuneration,

13:27.620 --> 13:33.620
Mr. Stone will make a selection largely on the basis of physical fitness.

13:33.620 --> 13:36.620
So you want to pass your physical with flying colors?

13:36.620 --> 13:45.620
Exactly. And unless I steer clear of you, I'm afraid those colors are going to be black and blue.

13:45.620 --> 13:52.620
So once again, Miss Brooks, I beg of you, go easy on me, won't you?

13:52.620 --> 13:58.620
Oh, you can depend on me, Mr. Conklin. Now you just relax, sir, and take it easy.

13:58.620 --> 14:01.620
I'll move this over where you can reach it and you won't have to...

14:01.620 --> 14:04.620
Aaaaaah!

14:04.620 --> 14:10.620
Oh, forgive me, sir. I'm so sorry. Did it... did it get your foot?

14:10.620 --> 14:14.620
That's quite all right.

14:14.620 --> 14:19.620
I have another one.

14:19.620 --> 14:23.620
Now, if you will kindly vacate the premises.

14:23.620 --> 14:26.620
Yes, Mr. Conklin.

14:26.620 --> 14:30.620
The tailor said your suit will be dry enough for pressing in about an hour, sir.

14:30.620 --> 14:31.620
Oh, hi, Miss Brooks.

14:31.620 --> 14:33.620
How are you, Mr. Barnum?

14:33.620 --> 14:35.620
Oh, I can't kick.

14:35.620 --> 14:37.620
Neither can Mr. Conklin.

14:37.620 --> 14:39.620
Of course, it was an accident, but you see, I was trying...

14:39.620 --> 14:45.620
Miss Brooks is preparing me for my physical examination.

14:45.620 --> 14:51.620
In view of my condition, Boynton, I hope you'll take a rain check on that dinner invitation for this evening.

14:51.620 --> 14:52.620
Of course, Mr. Conklin.

14:52.620 --> 14:57.620
It so happens Miss Brooks has offered to cook dinner for me tonight on my new stove.

14:57.620 --> 14:59.620
I bought it yesterday, as you know.

14:59.620 --> 15:02.620
Yes, yes. I was present at the time.

15:02.620 --> 15:08.620
You might be interested to learn that I called that appliance store a few moments ago, Miss Brooks.

15:08.620 --> 15:16.620
Now, don't ask me why, but I have decided to purchase a home freezing unit.

15:16.620 --> 15:18.620
Oh, splendid, Mr. Conklin.

15:18.620 --> 15:20.620
How big is your freezer?

15:20.620 --> 15:24.620
Don't build up your hopes, Miss Brooks.

15:24.620 --> 15:29.620
It's far too small to hold me.

15:29.620 --> 15:37.620
Oh, by the way, Boynton, I'd like to install it myself, so I wish you'd return that tool chest you borrowed.

15:37.620 --> 15:39.620
Yes, sir. It's still in the kitchen.

15:39.620 --> 15:42.620
Perhaps after dinner, Miss Brooks and I could return it to you.

15:42.620 --> 15:44.620
Never mind.

15:44.620 --> 15:48.620
I'll pick it up at your place after school.

15:48.620 --> 15:52.620
Now, if you folks will run along, I have to take my high blood pressure pill.

15:52.620 --> 15:55.620
Oh, here. Let me help you, Mr. Conklin.

15:55.620 --> 15:56.620
Here's your glass.

15:56.620 --> 15:58.620
Thank you.

15:58.620 --> 16:03.620
I'll drop round between five and six, Boynton.

16:03.620 --> 16:06.620
Say when, Mr. Conklin.

16:06.620 --> 16:24.620
When?

16:24.620 --> 16:28.620
When?

16:28.620 --> 16:37.620
Hurry, we can cross the border before midnight.

16:37.620 --> 16:39.620
Oh, we'd better hurry, Mrs. Davis.

16:39.620 --> 16:41.620
Mr. Boynton should be back in about 15 minutes.

16:41.620 --> 16:46.620
And I don't want him to discover why I insisted that he catch a matinee today.

16:46.620 --> 16:51.620
I think it was smart of you to get rid of the bashful goon,

16:51.620 --> 16:55.620
so I could sneak over and cook his dinner.

16:55.620 --> 17:00.620
Of course, after you get married, you'll have to tell him you're not much of a cook.

17:00.620 --> 17:03.620
By then, who cares?

17:03.620 --> 17:05.620
You're right, dear.

17:05.620 --> 17:12.620
After marriage, all a girl needs is a lipstick and a good can opener.

17:12.620 --> 17:17.620
And if she has the right lipstick, she doesn't even need a can opener.

17:17.620 --> 17:20.620
Oh, you certainly did a wonderful job on that turkey, Mrs. Davis.

17:20.620 --> 17:24.620
I started cooking it at home the minute you phoned me from school.

17:24.620 --> 17:27.620
We'll have to reheat it for about 10 minutes, though.

17:27.620 --> 17:28.620
Oh?

17:28.620 --> 17:29.620
Where are the matches?

17:29.620 --> 17:31.620
Oh, we don't need any matches.

17:31.620 --> 17:39.620
The booklet said that all these burners light automatically.

17:39.620 --> 17:40.620
There we are.

17:40.620 --> 17:44.620
Well, I'll set the temperature at 350 degrees.

17:44.620 --> 17:46.620
You think that'll be hot enough for you?

17:46.620 --> 17:51.620
Yes, until Mr. Boynton finishes dinner.

17:51.620 --> 17:54.620
Well, I'd better go in and finish setting the table now,

17:54.620 --> 17:57.620
and thanks for all the help you gave me, Mrs. Davis.

17:57.620 --> 18:01.620
Don't mention it, dear.

18:01.620 --> 18:09.620
Da-da-da-da-da-da-dum, da-dum, da-dum, da-dum, da-dum.

18:09.620 --> 18:13.620
He might get ideas if we watch some wrestling on TV.

18:13.620 --> 18:18.620
Oh, Connie, Connie, the light blew out in the kitchen.

18:18.620 --> 18:20.620
It's quite dark in there.

18:20.620 --> 18:23.020
Well, everything's practically set here, Mrs. Davis.

18:23.020 --> 18:27.360
I'll have Mr. Boynton change the bulb as soon as he gets in.

18:27.360 --> 18:29.900
Speak of the Boynton.

18:29.900 --> 18:32.860
Mrs. Davis, you've got to make a sneaky exit.

18:32.860 --> 18:36.500
If he sees you here, he'll never believe I cooked that dinner.

18:36.500 --> 18:37.400
Stand behind the door.

18:42.200 --> 18:43.100
Hi.

18:43.100 --> 18:44.340
Have you been waiting long?

18:44.340 --> 18:46.900
For years.

18:46.900 --> 18:49.060
I mean, just a few minutes, Mr. Boynton.

18:49.060 --> 18:51.220
See how prettily the table is set.

18:51.220 --> 18:52.540
Go ahead, take a look.

18:52.540 --> 18:53.460
I'll shut the door.

18:53.460 --> 18:54.260
Lovely.

18:54.260 --> 18:55.060
Here.

18:55.060 --> 18:55.860
Oh.

18:55.860 --> 18:56.660
Oh.

18:56.660 --> 18:57.460
Oh.

18:57.460 --> 18:58.260
Oh.

18:58.260 --> 19:02.860
When I closed it, I threw a slow curve.

19:02.860 --> 19:04.860
Did you catch a mad name, Mr. Boynton?

19:04.860 --> 19:07.460
No, I spent the time visiting with Mr. Grimaldi.

19:07.460 --> 19:09.260
He lives in the apartment directly below.

19:09.260 --> 19:11.740
His wife's expecting a baby any minute now.

19:11.740 --> 19:12.980
A baby.

19:12.980 --> 19:14.380
Oh, isn't that sweet?

19:14.380 --> 19:16.380
I just love babies.

19:16.380 --> 19:20.060
I guess that's because I'm the domestic type.

19:20.060 --> 19:23.180
That's probably why I like to cook, too.

19:23.180 --> 19:28.780
Someday, I'd just like to have a baby and cook.

19:28.780 --> 19:32.580
Not the same day, of course.

19:32.580 --> 19:35.380
Oh, I just finished cooking a turkey for you.

19:35.380 --> 19:36.180
What?

19:36.180 --> 19:38.060
A real, live turkey?

19:38.060 --> 19:40.380
He's dead now.

19:40.380 --> 19:44.940
That is, he didn't put up much of a fight when I stuffed him.

19:44.940 --> 19:46.980
I don't mean to sound ungrateful, Miss Brooks,

19:46.980 --> 19:50.180
but I wish you hadn't gone to all that expense on my account

19:50.180 --> 19:51.580
when you consider that I could have

19:51.580 --> 19:52.780
had dinner on Mr. Conklin.

19:52.780 --> 19:53.980
Oh, forget it, Mr. Boynton.

19:53.980 --> 19:55.380
It's my treat.

19:55.380 --> 19:57.780
I'm going to turn the oven down in a minute.

19:57.780 --> 20:00.380
Oh, by the way, the light burned out in the kitchen.

20:00.380 --> 20:01.780
Would you change the bulb for me?

20:01.780 --> 20:02.580
Sure thing.

20:02.580 --> 20:04.980
There's some more bulbs.

20:04.980 --> 20:06.980
Miss Brooks, when you turned the oven on,

20:06.980 --> 20:10.580
are you sure the match reached the gas jets?

20:10.580 --> 20:11.780
What match?

20:11.780 --> 20:14.980
The booklet said that all those burners lit automatically.

20:14.980 --> 20:16.580
Well, not till I can install them.

20:16.580 --> 20:18.380
Oh, let's get some fresh air in here.

20:18.380 --> 20:21.380
Kitchen's loaded with gas.

20:21.380 --> 20:23.980
No, we can't just let it go on escaping.

20:23.980 --> 20:24.980
I'll turn off the oven.

20:24.980 --> 20:25.980
Don't be foolish.

20:25.980 --> 20:27.580
You might stumble in that dark kitchen.

20:27.580 --> 20:28.980
We'd better call the fire department.

20:28.980 --> 20:30.180
They'll come over with masks.

20:30.180 --> 20:30.980
Uh-huh.

20:33.380 --> 20:34.980
Hello, operator.

20:34.980 --> 20:36.580
Oh, excuse me, Mr. Grimaldi, but you'll

20:36.580 --> 20:38.380
have to get off the line.

20:38.380 --> 20:41.180
But we...

20:41.180 --> 20:42.980
Mr. Grimaldi said he thinks the baby's due,

20:42.980 --> 20:44.780
and he's trying to reach the doctor.

20:44.780 --> 20:46.580
Well, I don't blame him.

20:46.580 --> 20:48.380
Look, Mr. Grimaldi, we're expecting

20:48.380 --> 20:49.980
a blessed event here, too.

20:49.980 --> 20:52.380
And I've got to call the fire department.

20:52.380 --> 20:56.380
Yes, I know, but, well, but you...

20:56.380 --> 20:59.380
He says that the nurse said she'd have the doctor on the line

20:59.380 --> 21:01.380
in just a few seconds.

21:01.380 --> 21:03.980
Well, we'll hang on so that we can place our call

21:03.980 --> 21:05.980
as soon as you hang up.

21:05.980 --> 21:08.380
Oh, and congratulations, Mr. Grimaldi.

21:08.380 --> 21:11.380
I hope you have a fireman.

21:11.380 --> 21:12.180
Yeah.

21:22.580 --> 21:25.380
Well, he must have stepped out for a minute.

21:25.380 --> 21:29.380
Let's see, where did he say he left that tool chest?

21:29.380 --> 21:30.180
The kitchen.

21:35.780 --> 21:37.380
Oh, great.

21:37.380 --> 21:38.380
No light.

21:49.380 --> 21:51.580
Darn matches haven't defrosted yet.

21:54.780 --> 21:59.380
Oh, there must be one dry one in this box.

21:59.380 --> 22:01.180
Well, we can't wait any longer.

22:01.180 --> 22:03.180
I'll have to use the pay phone down the hall.

22:03.180 --> 22:08.180
It's the worst day of my life.

22:08.180 --> 22:09.180
Oh, now.

22:14.380 --> 22:16.180
Somebody in the kitchen?

22:16.180 --> 22:19.180
It is I, Osgood Conklin.

22:49.180 --> 23:07.560
You were trying to tell me something, Miss Gertner?

23:07.560 --> 23:14.020
Just that the kitchen was loaded with gas, Miss Gertner.

23:14.020 --> 23:16.340
Thank you.

23:16.340 --> 23:23.340
I'll, uh, I'll be very careful.

23:23.340 --> 23:31.460
Well, what are you waiting for?

23:31.460 --> 23:34.460
You can still have dinner on Mr. Conklin.

23:34.460 --> 23:35.460
Daddy?

23:35.460 --> 23:39.460
In the inner office, Harriet.

23:39.460 --> 23:40.460
Oh.

23:40.460 --> 23:49.700
I just saw Miss Brooks in the dramatic club prop room about five minutes ago, Daddy, and

23:49.700 --> 23:50.700
I told her you wanted to see her.

23:50.700 --> 23:51.700
What did she say?

23:51.700 --> 23:52.700
She said she'd be right over.

23:52.700 --> 23:53.700
She seemed awfully nervous, though.

23:53.700 --> 23:54.700
She seemed nervous?

23:54.700 --> 23:55.700
How do you think I feel about her?

23:55.700 --> 23:56.700
Well, I'll be ready for this interview.

23:56.700 --> 24:16.700
Daddy, what in the world?

24:16.700 --> 24:29.420
It's too late to fire me, Mr. Conklin.

24:29.420 --> 24:30.420
I just canned myself.

24:30.420 --> 24:49.560
Bonnie, look at me.

24:49.560 --> 24:55.640
